p- or spoon-shaped scraping instrument), followed by electrodesiccation using an electric needle/current to cauterize/destroy residual cancer cells and control bleeding. These steps describe both the mechanical debulking component and the electrosurgical component that treats the lesion base. [Data: Entities (572)]\\n\\nThe relationship data reinforces the first step operationally by linking C&E to the “thin tool with sharp loop or spoon” used to scrape off the visible tumor. This anchors the instrument and its purpose within the procedure sequence. [Data: Entities (573, 572); Relationships (811)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Clinical decision boundary: deeper-than-expected tumors may require conversion to excision\",\"explanation\":\"The procedure description notes that while C&E is recommended for superficial, low-risk lesions, if the tumor is found to be deeper than expected, the approach may be changed to surgical excision to ensure adequate removal. This introduces an important decision point: intra-procedural assessment can shift management away from C&E when depth suggests that more definitive margin-controlled or deeper excision may be necessary. [Data: Entities (572)]\\n\\nThis decision boundary functionally limits C&E’s role to lesions that remain consistent with superficial/low-risk expectations, reinforcing the importance of patient and tumor selection and the need for flexibility in management when the clinical picture changes. [Data: Entities (572)]\"},{\"summary\":\"LOW-RISK CSCC management context: surgery-first with multiple procedural options; radiation as alternative if surgery not feasible\",\"explanation\":\"LOW-RISK CSCC is described as a lower-risk category of CSCC that is typically localized and has not spread to regional lymph nodes. Management is generally centered on surgery, with procedural options including C&E, standard surgical excision, or Mohs micrographic surgery/other forms of peripheral and deep en face margin assessment (Mohs/PDEMA). This p